Alright, the user wants the CLI to optionally show the agent’s internal thoughts, toggled via a `/thought-show` or `/thought-hide` command. To support that, the configuration schema must expose a boolean flag that the runtime can check to decide whether to render the `` blocks or hide them behind the spinner UI.

Looking at the existing codebase, the user‑config interface lives in `src/core/config.ts`. Earlier I already updated the `Effort` enum and its level map, so the file is being actively maintained. The current `UserConfig` definition is:

```ts
export interface UserConfig {
  diagnostics: boolean;
  effort: Effort;
  yolo: boolean;
  intro: boolean;
}
```

There’s no `thoughts` field here, which means any attempt to read `config.thoughts` will be a TypeScript error and the feature can’t be toggled at runtime. Adding the property is straightforward: just insert a new line before the closing brace, preserving the existing ordering and formatting.

I need to be careful with the edit operation:
- Use the `Edit` tool rather than `Write` because I’m only modifying a small snippet, not overwriting the whole file.
- Set `replace_all` to `false` so the tool only replaces the exact `old_string` block.
- Provide the exact `old_string` (the current interface block) and the `new_string` (the same block with an extra `thoughts: boolean;` line).

This will give the CLI a new config option, enable the UI to respect `/thought-show` / `/thought-hide`, and keep the rest of the file untouched. No other files need to be touched right now; the UI components that render thoughts will later read this flag.

